From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thelamer <ryankuba@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:56:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Code a row's run of constant macroblocks at once

The constant-macroblock skip coded each macroblock the caller's mb_info marks constant on its
own: a 16x16 copy of the reference, the intra border backup and the cache's right column stored
per macroblock, and x264_ratecontrol_mb called for each, about 70 ns a macroblock, which is what a
still 2560x1600 frame of 16000 of them spent its slices on. A run of them along a row is now coded
at once: the per-macroblock checks (the constant flag, the zero P_SKIP vector, the analysis QP
against the reference's effective QP) and the state its neighbours and the next frame read stay per
macroblock, the reference's pixels are copied for the run in one pass a row, the border backup is
one copy for the run, only the last macroblock's right column is cached, and the rate control
accumulates the run in one call (x264_ratecontrol_mb_skip_run). A row's first macroblock keeps the
full path, which sets the row's vector limits.

Measured with four sliced threads on four pinned cores of a Ryzen 9 5950X at 2560x1600 (CRF 25,
pixelflux's settings), a still frame took 0.27 ms where it took 0.41, a frame with a 256x256
change 0.56 where 0.68, and a full-screen scroll the same. Every stream comes out bit for bit the
same: CRF at one and four threads and VBV at one, 1080p and 2560x1600, a scroll, a moved window, a
small change, a still screen, forced IDRs, and frames without mb_info (126 runs), the version SEI
left out.
